>Just because I am a Wizard does not mean that I must have a neutral alignment.
>I'm sure chaotic and lawful Wizards would also make sense.  I suppose that
>a forced alignment makes sense for some characters, such as Knights; but for
>most of the character classes I think the player should be able to choose
>his/her own alignment.  Priests/Priestesses (spelling?) are another example;
>they should be able to have at least a lawful or chaotic alignment [...]

	I agree completely - as I always understood it "lawful" and "chaotic"
had nothing to do with whether you were "good" or "evil", but rather with
how consistently you were one or the other. A "chaotic" character is one
who may be a bit unpredictable, due to a total lack of fixed morals (be they
"good" or "evil" morals) A lot of the old D&D games I used to master were
based on the ideas of "chaotic good", "chaotic evil", "lawful evil", etc.
I don't know how such a thing would map to hack - would a lawful evil
character lose points if he didn't kill his dog ?  :-)  (or gain them if
he did - I *LIKE* it !)

	My main bitches, conceptually, with nethack as it stands now are:
	1) if you are EATING and someone starts shooting you with a crossbow,
		or whacking you with a sword, you STOP real fast. If you
		want to make it so you can't *fight* while you're eating,
		fine, but you should be able to *run* until you can put that
		jackal drumstick down and prepare your weapons.

	1.a) if you are sitting resting and someone starts shooting you with
		a crossbow, you STOP. I've gotten creamed by critters that
		throw stuff while I'm resting. If I throw something at a
		monster it wakes up immediately. I should get the same
		ability.

	2) how many people choke to death of overeating in hack ? how many
		in real life ? 'nuff said. If it comes to that, penalize the
		person by making them slow because of all the jackalburgers.

	3) I'd like an option to turn off the dog/cat, though watching the
		little bastard starve *is* fun.

